Without a prior sanity check, `__va()` converts a physical address that may lie outside the usable system RAM (due to a limiting `mem=` parameter) into a virtual address. Accessing this virtual address causes a page fault and kernel panic.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
ret = ima_validate_range(ima_kexec_buffer_phys, ima_kexec_buffer_size);
if (ret)
    return ret;
*addr = __va(ima_kexec_buffer_phys);
Reason:
The added call to `ima_validate_range()` verifies that the physical address range of the IMA measurement list falls within the system RAM. If validation fails, the function returns an error, preventing the unsafe `__va()` call and avoiding the page fault.

